adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the auth check is:
```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```
If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is not set in the environment (so `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is `undefined`) and the request also omits the header (so `req.headers[...]` is `undefined`), then `undefined !== undefined` is `false` and the request is accepted, effectively bypassing the webhook-secret protection when misconfigured. The handler does not enforce that `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` must be defined/non-empty before allowing access (it only checks `APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED` first).

In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the code decrypts and parses untrusted input without error handling:

```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
```

Both `symmetricDecrypt(...)` (e.g., bad/invalid ciphertext, wrong key, empty key) and `JSON.parse(...)` (non-JSON plaintext) can throw. There is no surrounding `try/catch` (nor validation that `CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` is set), so such cases will bubble up and cause an unhandled 500 in this Next.js API route rather than a controlled 4xx response. This is a concrete defect introduced by the diff and is directly verifiable from the code shown.
